        rt.kind[90] = 1; rt.h[90] = 3; rt.h[91] = 0;
        rt.h[92] = 100; rt.h[93] = 101; rt.h[94] = 200; rt.h[95] = 201;
        rt.kind[120] = 1; rt.h[120] = 2; rt.h[121] = 4;
        rt.h[122] = 500; rt.h[123] = 501; rt.h[140] = 210;
        rt.kind[210] = 1; rt.h[210] = 3; rt.h[211] = 0;
        rt.h[212] = 100; rt.h[213] = 101; rt.h[214] = 400; rt.h[215] = 401;
        rt.kind[240] = 1; rt.h[240] = 0; rt.h[241] = 2; rt.h[260] = 270;
        rt.kind[270] = 1; rt.h[270] = 7; rt.h[271] = 0;
        rt.h[272] = 100; rt.h[273] = 101; rt.h[274] = 400; rt.h[275] = 401;
        rt.h[276] = 600; rt.h[277] = 601;
        rt.kind[150] = 0; rt.h[150] = 0x55; rt.h[151] = 3;
        rt.h[152] = 100; rt.h[153] = 101; rt.h[154] = 200; rt.h[155] = 201;
        rt.h[156] = 300; rt.h[157] = 301;
        rt.kind[180] = 0; rt.h[180] = 0x55; rt.h[181] = 2;
        rt.h[182] = 100; rt.h[183] = 101; rt.h[184] = 200; rt.h[185] = 201;
        var o = new StringBuilder();
        long a = NodeDissoc(rt, 1, 0, 0x01, 100, NIL);
        o.Append(a).Append(':').Append(rt.champAdded ? 1 : 0).Append(' ');
        long b = NodeDissoc(rt, 30, 0, 0x01, 100, NIL);
        o.Append(b).Append(':').Append(rt.champAdded ? 1 : 0).Append(' ');
        long c = NodeDissoc(rt, 30, 0, 0x01, 999, NIL);
        o.Append(c).Append(':').Append(rt.champAdded ? 1 : 0).Append(' ');
        long d = NodeDissoc(rt, 60, 0, 0x01, 100, NIL);
        o.Append(d).Append(':').Append(rt.champAdded ? 1 : 0).Append(' ');
        long g2 = NodeDissoc(rt, 120, 0, 0x02, 100, NIL);
        o.Append(g2).Append(':').Append(rt.champAdded ? 1 : 0).Append(' ');
        long h2 = NodeDissoc(rt, 240, 0, 0x01, 100, NIL);
        o.Append(h2).Append(':').Append(rt.champAdded ? 1 : 0).Append(' ');
        long e = NodeDissoc(rt, 30, 0, 0x1F, 100, NIL);
        o.Append(e).Append(':').Append(rt.champAdded ? 1 : 0).Append(' ');
        long f = NodeDissoc(rt, 150, 0, 0x55, 999, NIL);
        o.Append(f).Append(':').Append(rt.champAdded ? 1 : 0).Append(' ');
        long g = CollDissoc(rt, 150, 200, NIL);
        o.Append(g).Append(':').Append(rt.champAdded ? 1 : 0).Append(' ');
        for (int k = (int) g; k < (int) g + 6; k++) o.Append(rt.h[k]).Append(',');
        long i = CollDissoc(rt, 180, 100, NIL);
        o.Append(' ').Append(i).Append(':').Append(rt.champAdded ? 1 : 0).Append(' ');
        for (int k = (int) i; k < (int) i + 4; k++) o.Append(rt.h[k]).Append(',');
        rt.starve = true;
        long j = CollDissoc(rt, 150, 200, NIL);
        o.Append(" oom=").Append(j).Append(" roots=").Append(rt.roots.Count)
         .Append(" log=").Append(rt.log);
        Console.WriteLine(o.ToString());
    }
--expect
300:1 340:1 30:0 380:1 460:1 540:1 30:0 150:0 580:1 85,2,100,101,300,301, 620:1 16777216,0,200,201, oom=65535 roots=0 log=BRBBIRNCBx
--expect-why
DERIVED BY HAND from `dissoc.kin` over the hand-laid nodes in the probe.
Nothing here was read off a run; the arithmetic for each field is below.

HOW TO READ IT. Each `addr:flag` pair is the Value answered and
`champ-added`, which "reads as CHANGED on this path". The fixture's `obj`
allocates 40 bytes from bump = 300 AND APPENDS A LETTER TO `log`, so the
addresses and the log spell out which helper ran, in order:

  B `bn-new`   R `bn-copy-remove-entry`   N `bn-copy-set-node`
  I `bn-node-to-inline`   C `cn-new`   x an allocation refused
  addresses: 300, 340, 380, 420, 460, 500, 540, 580, 620 in call order.

Also: `bitpos h shift` = 1 << ((h >> shift) & 31), `index-of bm bit` =
popcount(bm & (bit - 1)), and the fixture's `hash-value v` = (v * 7) & 0xff.

`300:1` -- COLLAPSE. Node A has datamap 6 (two entries) and no sub-nodes.
bit = bitpos(0x01, 0) = 1 << 1 = 2; 6 & 2 is set, so at = popcount(6 & 1) = 0,
and key 100 matches. popcount(dm) == 2 and nm == 0, so the SHAPE INVARIANT
fires: "a node must never be left holding one entry and nothing else". It
builds a fresh single-entry node (the first B, at 300) holding the OTHER pair,
(200,201). At shift 0 the new datamap is dm XOR bit = 4; the answer is the new
node's address, not A's.

`340:1` -- NO COLLAPSE. Node B has three entries, so dropping one leaves two
and the invariant is satisfied: the plain `bn-copy-remove-entry` path, which is
the R at 340. The contrast with the field above is the whole test -- same key,
same bit, different entry count, different helper.

`30:0` -- THE SLOT HOLDS A DIFFERENT KEY. Bit 2 is in B's datamap, but the key
there is 100 and the caller asked for 999. `val-eq` says no, so nothing is
allocated, `champ-added` is FALSE, and the node comes back UNCHANGED by
identity -- 30, the address passed in. A dissoc of an absent key must not
allocate, or every miss churns the heap.

`380:1` -- RECURSIVE COLLAPSE THAT REPLACES THE PARENT. C has no entries and
exactly one sub-node (node 90, two entries). The recursion into 90 collapses it
to a single-entry node (the B at 380), and this time the new datamap is
computed from the SURVIVING KEY'S hash rather than by clearing a bit -- shift
is 5, not 0, so `newdm = bitpos(hash-value 200, 0)`. The fixture's
hash-value(200) is (200 * 7) & 0xff = 1400 & 0xff = 120, and 120 & 31 = 24, so
newdm = 1 << 24 = 16777216. That number reappears below.
Back in the parent: the child's size class is 1, and the parent has dm == 0
with one sub-node, so "nothing else lives here, so this node IS the child now"
  -- the answer is 380, the child itself, and C disappears.

`460:1` -- THE SAME COLLAPSE, FOLDED INLINE INSTEAD. G has ONE entry of its own
(500,501) plus one sub-node (210). The recursion collapses 210 to a
single-entry node (the B at 420, datamap 1 << (hash-value(400) & 31) = 1 << 16
= 65536), but now the parent is not empty, so the collapsed child's pair is
folded back into G's entry half: `bn-node-to-inline`, the I at 460. Two
adjacent fields, the same collapse underneath, two different parent answers --
which is exactly the distinction the source's `dm == 0 && popcount(nm) == 1`
test makes.

`540:1` -- THE COMMON CASE. H's child (270) has THREE entries, so removing one
leaves a node that does NOT collapse: its size class is not 1, and the parent
rebuilds itself around the new child with `bn-copy-set-node` -- the R at 500
then the N at 540. This is the only path in the probe that reaches
`bn-copy-set-node` at all.

`30:0` again -- THE BIT IS IN NEITHER MAP. h = 0x1F gives bit = 1 << 31, and
B's datamap is 14 and nodemap 0, so neither test matches. The final `else`:
`champ-added` false and the node returned unchanged. The top bit is chosen on
purpose -- it is the bit position a signed shift would get wrong.

`150:0` -- A COLLISION NODE REACHED THROUGH `node-dissoc`. `is-bmnode` is
false, so it is handed straight to `coll-dissoc`, which scans all three pairs
for key 999, finds none, and answers `found == cnt` -- the SENTINEL the header
describes, "cnt is already outside the valid range". Unchanged, flag 0.

`580:1` then `85,2,100,101,300,301,` -- A PLAIN COLLISION REMOVAL. Key 200 is
found at index 1 of three. cnt != 2, so a fresh collision node one pair shorter
(the C at 580) is built with the SAME hash: the dumped slots are
  hash 0x55 = 85, count 2, then (100,101) and (300,301).
Index 1 was skipped and index 2 SHIFTED DOWN into its place -- `d` advances
only on a copy. `85,2,100,101,200,201,` would mean it removed the wrong pair;
`85,2,100,101,0,0,` would mean the shift never happened.

`620:1` then `16777216,0,200,201,` -- A COLLISION NODE DOWN TO ONE PAIR. F has
two pairs and key 100 is removed, so cnt == 2 and it must "become a
single-entry BITMAP node so the parent can fold it back inline" -- the B at
620. Its datamap is `bitpos(hash-value 200, 0)` = 1 << 24 = 16777216, the same
number derived in the `380` case because the surviving key is the same 200.
Then nodemap 0 and the surviving pair (200,201). A collision node of one pair
would be the invariant violation this branch exists to prevent.

`oom=65535 roots=0 log=BRBBIRNCBx`:
  65535 = 0xFFFF = NIL. With `starve` set, `cn-new` refuses, and `coll-dissoc`
  answers NIL rather than writing into address 0 -- note it had ALREADY set
  `champ-added` true before the allocation, so a caller must not read that
  flag as proof of success.
  roots = 0: every `mark`/`pop-to` balanced, including the refusal path, which
  pops before returning NIL.
  log = BRBBIRNCBx is the allocation sequence spelled out: B (collapse),
  R (plain remove), B (child collapse), B then I (child collapse folded
  inline), R then N (child changed, parent re-noded), C (collision shrink),
  B (collision collapse), x (refused). Ten helper calls in that exact order --
  the field that would catch a right-looking answer reached by the wrong route,
  since several of these paths can produce a plausible address while running a
  different helper.
